we're trying to build them now for co-packaged  

116
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:44,879
optics and even other form factors. Let me 
try to come at it from a few different angles,  

117
00:12:44,879 --> 00:12:52,159
because everyone is coming from their own 
understanding and you have to map it to a  

118
00:12:52,159 --> 00:12:57,679
few things. One of the first things to realize 
is that we say we're building an optical engine.  

119
00:12:58,559 --> 00:13:05,919
Our optical engine is a combination of a 
leading-edge advanced node electrical IC  

120
00:13:05,919 --> 00:13:12,159
(EIC). Right now we build in the TSMC COUPE 
process, where the EIC is a three-nanometer  

121
00:13:12,159 --> 00:13:28,719
IC with hundreds of millions of transistors. So 
you should think of this as photonics adding a  

122
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:36,399
new high-performing, leading-edge advanced 
capability to leading-edge CMOS nodes and  

123
00:13:36,399 --> 00:13:42,720
leading-edge advanced packages. The first 
thing to realize is that people generally know  

124
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:48,213
that leading-edge CMOS is hard and advanced 
packaging is hard. People know about CoWoS,  

125
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:57,200
the supply chain shortages, and the challenges 
in getting these large-package, multi-die  

126
00:13:57,200 --> 00:14:04,640
AI accelerators manufactured and produced. You 
should just bootstrap into that understanding  

127
00:14:05,759 --> 00:14:15,840
that a large multi-die package AI accelerator 
is a difficult thing to manufacture and  

128
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:21,840
build even without optics, okay? So now we're 
going to say I'm going to take this large  

129
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:30,720
leading-edge substrate, multi-reticle advanced 
package, and add an optical engine. The optical  

130
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:38,480
engine is a combination of leading-edge EICs 
and a new photonics wafer manufacturing process  

131
00:14:38,480 --> 00:14:45,200
flow. The new thing I have to do there is also 
attach an optical connector to this package.

132
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:47,200
Mark Wade (14:36.29)
I think that's the easiest  

133
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:54,160
way to see it, at least for people coming 
from the understanding that multi-reticle,  

134
00:14:54,159 --> 00:15:02,480
large package size compute accelerators are 
difficult to build. Now you're adding a new  

135
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:11,519
advanced capability into that package. Difficult 
on difficult makes it more difficult. So why is  

136
00:15:11,519 --> 00:15:17,360
everyone doing this? Why is everyone so motivated? 
It's because we've really hit the physical limits  

137
00:15:19,039 --> 00:15:27,519
to escape the amount of bandwidth and the radix of 
connectivity out of these large packages. You've  

138
00:15:27,519 --> 00:15:33,759
got multiple technologies within that package 
that are fully pushed up against their physical  

139
00:15:33,759 --> 00:15:40,639
bounds. You need a new feature that can come in 
and open up a new degree of freedom of scaling.  

140
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:50,800
And that's just on the CMOS and large package 
size. There are things to talk about on the  

141
00:15:50,799 --> 00:15:56,799
