makes optical connectivity exiting the package  

157
00:17:51,119 --> 00:17:56,639
look like something that can be high-volume 
scaled through a CMOS-like manufacturing flow  

158
00:17:56,640 --> 00:18:02,320
is unquestioned. It allows customers to start 
thinking of optical I/O within the context of  

159
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:16,320
their foundry OSAT model of the world. Once we get 
to that world, it simplifies a lot of the product  

160
00:18:16,319 --> 00:18:25,359
design, form factors, and configurations and 
choices you have to make. So I do think there's  

161
00:18:26,559 --> 00:18:34,240
a trajectory as we get past this crunch in the 
next three, four, or five years. When we land in  

162
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:41,039
a world where optical connectivity is just viewed 
as an advanced feature coming from a foundry,  

163
00:18:41,839 --> 00:18:51,199
that simplifies the world a lot for people. You 
can get out of this chaotic land of, "Is it a  

164
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:58,559
pluggable? Is it a board-integrated component? 
What's the form factor? What's the physical  

165
00:18:58,559 --> 00:19:05,440
technology inside? What supply chain is delivering 
it?" There are a lot of permutations you have to  

166
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:14,320
manage to simply overcome the fact that you can't 
get this from your CMOS foundry. So I think the  

167
00:19:14,319 --> 00:19:25,519
longer-term view—let's say 2030 and beyond—starts 
to simplify and get easier. But in the short term,  

168
00:19:25,519 --> 00:19:34,240
when the reality is that these things are not 
fully available in high-volume production today,  

169
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:41,759
it forces everyone to scramble to find 
their 2027 or 2028 stopgap solution.

170
00:19:41,759 --> 00:19:46,720
Mark Wade (19:32.995)
That triggers a full exploration of the  

171
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:55,839
